course: dictionary
lesson: 13051
title: Fatigued: very tired, or weakened metal
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are more formal than everyday talk. «After the marathon, the runner was fatigued and could barely stand.» Fatigued is a grown-up way of saying very tired. The clues barely stand and the long race tell you that.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence. «The runner was very tired and worn out and could barely stand» still makes sense, so you have the right meaning. Fatigued is a little stronger and more serious than tired.
explain: The same word can have a special meaning in science. «The bridge cracked because its metal was fatigued by years of heavy traffic.» Here fatigued means weakened by being bent or loaded again and again. Look at the subject: a bridge is made of metal, not a person.
fact: Fatigued means very tired, worn out in body or mind.
fact: Fatigued is a formal word. Everyday words for it are tired, worn out, exhausted and knackered (UK slang).
fact: In science, metal that is fatigued has been weakened by being bent or loaded again and again.
fact: Metal fatigue can make planes or bridges crack, so engineers check them carefully.
fact: Fatigue is the root word: fatigue, fatigues, fatigued and fatiguing all come from it.
exercise: «After the long match, the players were fatigued and sat down to rest.» What does fatigued mean? | Hungry for lunch | Full of energy and ready to run | *Very tired and worn out | Cold and wet from the rain
exercise: Engineers check bridges because metal that is fatigued can crack. What does fatigued mean for metal? | Painted a bright colour | *Weakened by being bent or loaded again and again | Warm from the sun | Shiny and smooth from polishing
exercise: «The night shift left the nurse fatigued and slow.» Find and copy one word that means very tired. | shift | *fatigued | night | left
test: Which everyday word means about the same as fatigued? | Hungry | Loud | *Worn out | Happy
test: Why might a writer choose fatigued instead of tired? | It is only used by children | It means it is raining | *It is a more formal word that sounds serious | It means the same as hungry
exam: Which sentence uses fatigued correctly? | The bread was fatigued and warm. | The sky was fatigued with blue. | *The runner was fatigued after the race. | The pencil was fatigued and sharp.
